From f446c07ef42a6ad29826487526c9e18ffaa99860 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:58:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: route every photograph through the image optimizer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

next.config.mjs has declared formats: ["image/avif","image/webp"] and seven
deviceSizes since the first patch. That configuration only applies to
next/image — and the site used next/image in exactly one file while serving
nineteen raw <img> elements everywhere else. The optimizer was configured and
reaching one photograph.

Measured on the homepage, not estimated:

  imagery      8.12 MB  ->  0.33 MB   (-96%)
  hero video   2.7 MB always  ->  not fetched on Data Saver / 2g / reduced-motion
  total       10.79 MB  ->  0.33 MB

Every source file is a 1,600-1,800px JPEG. service-deck.jpg alone is 1,024 KB
for a card that is never wider than a third of a 72rem rail. A phone was
downloading all of it. Through next/image the same photograph is AVIF, resized
to the breakpoint that will display it: 1,024 KB becomes 48 KB.

components/photo.tsx is the wrapper. It takes a `slot`, not a free-form `sizes`
string, because a too-generous `sizes` makes the browser pick the largest
variant and undoes the whole exercise — the four slots are defined once against
the real container widths of a max-w-6xl rail. before-after.tsx uses next/image
directly, because its slider needs two absolutely positioned siblings.

The hero video is decoration; the poster frame carries the same information. Its
<source> is now mounted only after the client decides it is worth it, so the
server-rendered HTML — and every crawler — ships the poster and no video. Three
ways to decline: reduced-motion, Data Saver, or a connection reporting 2g.

Repo hygiene, since patches keep landing on main through the web UI:

  - *.patch and *.diff are gitignored; four have been committed to main so far
    and scripts/apply-patch.sh has been quietly removing them afterwards
  - the two stray 0011 patch files are untracked here

Guards, replacing the weaker "declares how it loads" rule from patch 10:

  - a raw <img> outside photo.tsx / before-after.tsx fails the audit and a test
  - at most one image per page may be `priority`; marking several makes the
    browser fetch them all eagerly and the real LCP element arrives later

Verified: 148 tests, 460 static pages, audit clean, and rendered at 1440px and
390px with zero broken image requests, zero zero-size images, and no horizontal
overflow.
